1
00:00:00,862 --> 00:00:03,224
Last episode we finished the rulebook, and

2
00:00:03,224 --> 00:00:05,432
used it to produce six paper entries.

3
00:00:06,174 --> 00:00:08,474
But six files sitting in one folder is not

4
00:00:08,474 --> 00:00:11,294
a knowledge base. It's a pile.

5
00:00:12,037 --> 00:00:13,387
In this episode we turn it

6
00:00:13,387 --> 00:00:14,582
into a real wiki.

7
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:18,216
First, let's be clear about how a pile of

8
00:00:18,216 --> 00:00:19,786
files differs from a wiki.

9
00:00:20,528 --> 00:00:22,303
The difference is not how many files you

10
00:00:22,303 --> 00:00:25,078
have. It is how many pages you have to

11
00:00:25,078 --> 00:00:27,086
touch when one new paper arrives.

12
00:00:27,828 --> 00:00:30,403
In a pile, every paper is its own file and

13
00:00:30,403 --> 00:00:31,648
nothing connects them.

14
00:00:32,391 --> 00:00:34,661
Add one, and you have one more file.

15
00:00:35,403 --> 00:00:37,791
In a wiki, a new paper brings new evidence

16
00:00:37,791 --> 00:00:40,241
to certain topics, and all of those pages

17
00:00:40,241 --> 00:00:41,473
have to change with it.

18
00:00:42,216 --> 00:00:44,311
So this episode does three things.

19
00:00:45,053 --> 00:00:46,311
Grow the middle layer.

20
00:00:47,053 --> 00:00:48,623
Define what update means.

21
00:00:49,366 --> 00:00:51,536
And finish the links between pages.

22
00:00:52,909 --> 00:00:54,429
This is the starting point.

23
00:00:55,171 --> 00:00:56,766
The index is one table.

24
00:00:57,509 --> 00:01:00,334
Six papers laid flat, and clicking any row

25
00:01:00,334 --> 00:01:01,966
takes you straight to an entry.

26
00:01:02,709 --> 00:01:04,784
The entries themselves are full, but there

27
00:01:04,784 --> 00:01:06,541
is nothing between the pages.

28
00:01:07,284 --> 00:01:08,691
So the first prompt is:

29
00:01:08,721 --> 00:01:11,116
turn the index into domains and topics.

30
00:01:11,859 --> 00:01:13,734
And then one more sentence that matters a

31
00:01:13,734 --> 00:01:16,296
lot. Tell me how you plan to do

32
00:01:16,296 --> 00:01:18,554
it. Don't start yet.

33
00:01:19,928 --> 00:01:21,803
The AI did not hand you a plan straight

34
00:01:21,803 --> 00:01:24,628
away. The first thing it did was scan

35
00:01:24,628 --> 00:01:26,553
every link already buried in those six

36
00:01:26,553 --> 00:01:28,591
entries, to see which concepts get

37
00:01:28,591 --> 00:01:30,323
mentioned and how they overlap.

38
00:01:31,066 --> 00:01:33,491
The result: the six entries already carry

39
00:01:33,491 --> 00:01:35,828
nine concept links with no page behind

40
00:01:35,828 --> 00:01:38,716
them. The FlyCircuit database is linked

41
00:01:38,716 --> 00:01:41,466
from all six. Standard brain and

42
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:43,248
registration, from four.

43
00:01:43,991 --> 00:01:45,928
In other words, the topics were not

44
00:01:45,928 --> 00:01:48,828
invented. They surfaced while the entries

45
00:01:48,828 --> 00:01:51,453
were being written. There is a cheap habit

46
00:01:51,453 --> 00:01:54,578
hiding in here. Bury a few more concept

47
00:01:54,578 --> 00:01:56,728
links while you write an entry, and later

48
00:01:56,728 --> 00:01:57,716
they become the evidence

49
00:01:57,716 --> 00:01:58,773
for your structure.

50
00:02:00,148 --> 00:02:01,868
Its plan had three layers.

51
00:02:02,611 --> 00:02:05,311
Domain, topic, concept, with the paper

52
00:02:05,311 --> 00:02:06,518
entries at the bottom.

53
00:02:07,261 --> 00:02:09,123
Then it stopped on its own and listed two

54
00:02:09,123 --> 00:02:10,606
things it should not decide.

55
00:02:11,348 --> 00:02:13,348
One, whether this domain should be called

56
00:02:13,348 --> 00:02:15,298
the fruit-fly connectome, or something

57
00:02:15,298 --> 00:02:17,173
broader that covers the imaging work as

58
00:02:17,173 --> 00:02:20,843
well. Two, who decides the concept list.

59
00:02:21,585 --> 00:02:23,511
Its own words: this list should be

60
00:02:23,511 --> 00:02:25,786
reviewed by a person, not decided by the

61
00:02:25,786 --> 00:02:28,581
Agent alone. Your answer was:

62
00:02:28,623 --> 00:02:30,768
keep the name, we can split it later.

63
00:02:31,511 --> 00:02:33,973
Cut three layers down to two, five topics

64
00:02:33,973 --> 00:02:37,048
is enough. And one more sentence that set

65
00:02:37,048 --> 00:02:38,906
the tone for every page after it.

66
00:02:39,648 --> 00:02:41,018
This is for me to read.

67
00:02:41,761 --> 00:02:43,281
I'm not chasing traffic.

68
00:02:44,664 --> 00:02:46,109
This is worth a pause.

69
00:02:46,851 --> 00:02:49,514
The AI had read every entry, so nobody had

70
00:02:49,514 --> 00:02:52,801
better data. It proposed three layers, and

71
00:02:52,801 --> 00:02:55,726
you cut it to two. That is not a mistake

72
00:02:55,726 --> 00:02:58,764
in its arithmetic. It simply does not have

73
00:02:58,764 --> 00:03:00,464
your picture of where this knowledge base

74
00:03:00,464 --> 00:03:03,139
is going. The structure is set by a

75
00:03:03,139 --> 00:03:06,339
person. The AI offers the options,

76
00:03:06,339 --> 00:03:08,609
measures the evidence, and does the work.

77
00:03:09,351 --> 00:03:11,764
And those four words, don't start yet, put

78
00:03:11,764 --> 00:03:13,709
these decisions before the output.

79
00:03:15,091 --> 00:03:18,129
Now the work starts. The paper entries

80
00:03:18,129 --> 00:03:20,241
move into their own folder, and the topic

81
00:03:20,241 --> 00:03:22,366
pages get English filenames, so the

82
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:24,411
technical terms can't be misread.

83
00:03:25,154 --> 00:03:26,799
The move came with good news.

84
00:03:27,541 --> 00:03:29,811
Obsidian resolves its links by filename,

85
00:03:29,841 --> 00:03:32,854
not by path. So all seventy-something

86
00:03:32,854 --> 00:03:34,729
cross-links between entries survived

87
00:03:34,729 --> 00:03:37,841
untouched. Only the image paths and the

88
00:03:37,841 --> 00:03:39,686
PDF links needed fixing.

89
00:03:40,429 --> 00:03:42,904
Then, instead of writing five topic pages

90
00:03:42,904 --> 00:03:44,991
at once, it wrote one and showed it to

91
00:03:44,991 --> 00:03:47,841
you. And it spelled out how that page was

92
00:03:47,841 --> 00:03:50,854
written. Seven sections, the style it

93
00:03:50,854 --> 00:03:52,491
used, and every number linked

94
00:03:52,491 --> 00:03:53,661
back to an entry.

95
00:03:55,047 --> 00:03:57,934
Why one page first? Because getting the

96
00:03:57,934 --> 00:04:00,379
style wrong costs very different amounts.

97
00:04:01,122 --> 00:04:04,004
One pilot page, and you rewrite one page.

98
00:04:04,747 --> 00:04:07,204
Five at once, and you rewrite five.

99
00:04:07,947 --> 00:04:10,259
It is the same principle as last episode's

100
00:04:10,259 --> 00:04:12,554
ask first, write the rulebook later.

101
00:04:13,297 --> 00:04:15,297
The smallest possible cost to check that

102
00:04:15,297 --> 00:04:16,909
the direction is right, before

103
00:04:16,909 --> 00:04:18,204
the output piles up.

104
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:21,665
You said, good, finish it.

105
00:04:22,408 --> 00:04:24,258
And the other four pages came out in the

106
00:04:24,258 --> 00:04:27,583
same shape. The index changed from a table

107
00:04:27,583 --> 00:04:29,858
of six papers into one domain with five

108
00:04:29,858 --> 00:04:33,420
topics. Each topic page carries a line at

109
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:35,220
the top recording which entries it draws

110
00:04:35,220 --> 00:04:38,195
on. That way you can always work backwards

111
00:04:38,195 --> 00:04:40,845
and ask: this new paper, which topics does

112
00:04:40,845 --> 00:04:43,920
it change? At the same time it went back

113
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,808
and added a rule. Handling one paper

114
00:04:46,808 --> 00:04:48,490
touches more than one page.

115
00:04:49,878 --> 00:04:52,160
So what actually goes on a topic page?

116
00:04:52,903 --> 00:04:54,960
Not a summary of the six entries.

117
00:04:55,703 --> 00:04:57,265
What goes there is what you cannot see

118
00:04:57,265 --> 00:04:58,998
inside any single entry.

119
00:04:59,740 --> 00:05:01,728
Three versions of the dataset lined up in

120
00:05:01,728 --> 00:05:04,678
one table. The same quantity, written

121
00:05:04,678 --> 00:05:06,385
differently in different papers.

122
00:05:07,128 --> 00:05:09,540
One paper saying a thing is possible, and

123
00:05:09,540 --> 00:05:11,385
the next one using it as fact.

124
00:05:12,128 --> 00:05:14,003
It only surfaces when they are side by

125
00:05:14,003 --> 00:05:16,990
side. And that surplus is the reason the

126
00:05:16,990 --> 00:05:18,510
topic page exists.

127
00:05:19,898 --> 00:05:21,523
Now the most practical part of this

128
00:05:21,523 --> 00:05:24,305
episode. Your instruction is:

129
00:05:24,385 --> 00:05:26,010
from now on I will keep dropping new

130
00:05:26,010 --> 00:05:28,298
papers into the papers folder, and every

131
00:05:28,298 --> 00:05:30,573
time I say update, go and find the ones

132
00:05:30,573 --> 00:05:31,868
you haven't processed.

133
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:34,510
The AI turned that into one sentence

134
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:37,510
first. The papers folder stops being a

135
00:05:37,510 --> 00:05:39,393
copy and becomes an inbox.

136
00:05:40,135 --> 00:05:42,330
You drop things in, it claims them.

137
00:05:43,073 --> 00:05:44,580
Then it did three things.

138
00:05:45,323 --> 00:05:47,410
It built a registry, and back-filled the

139
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:49,118
six papers already done.

140
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:51,735
It wrote a small program that scans the

141
00:05:51,735 --> 00:05:54,205
folder, compares it against the registry,

142
00:05:54,310 --> 00:05:56,180
and prints out what hasn't been handled.

143
00:05:56,923 --> 00:05:58,235
And it wrote the whole procedure

144
00:05:58,235 --> 00:05:59,280
into the rulebook.

145
00:06:00,645 --> 00:06:02,208
There is a design choice here worth

146
00:06:02,208 --> 00:06:05,365
looking at. Why keep a registry at all?

147
00:06:06,108 --> 00:06:07,970
Why not just check whether a file has a

148
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:10,928
matching entry? The answer it gave:

149
00:06:10,933 --> 00:06:12,720
guessing from filenames might be right

150
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,833
today, and wrong the moment you change how

151
00:06:14,833 --> 00:06:17,670
you name things. And there is one thing a

152
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:19,215
folder can never tell you.

153
00:06:19,958 --> 00:06:22,265
I read this paper and decided to skip it.

154
00:06:23,008 --> 00:06:25,290
So the registry carries a second table,

155
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:27,490
just for the ones deliberately skipped.

156
00:06:28,233 --> 00:06:30,633
An explicit table turns already processed

157
00:06:30,633 --> 00:06:32,115
from a guess into a fact.

158
00:06:33,497 --> 00:06:35,384
With the rules written, the test arrived

159
00:06:35,384 --> 00:06:38,359
immediately. You dropped five new papers

160
00:06:38,359 --> 00:06:39,742
in and said one word.

161
00:06:40,484 --> 00:06:43,609
Update. And the AI ran the whole procedure

162
00:06:43,609 --> 00:06:46,222
by itself. Find what hasn't been

163
00:06:46,222 --> 00:06:48,454
processed, handle them one at a time,

164
00:06:48,522 --> 00:06:50,504
register each one the moment it's done.

165
00:06:51,247 --> 00:06:53,217
You never had to restate the steps,

166
00:06:53,259 --> 00:06:54,822
because those four steps live in the

167
00:06:54,822 --> 00:06:56,797
rulebook, and the rulebook is present

168
00:06:56,797 --> 00:06:59,859
every round. You say one word, and it

169
00:06:59,859 --> 00:07:01,392
knows what that word means.

170
00:07:02,772 --> 00:07:04,910
The five papers together run to more than

171
00:07:04,910 --> 00:07:06,792
six hundred thousand characters.

172
00:07:07,535 --> 00:07:09,772
Push all of that into one conversation and

173
00:07:09,772 --> 00:07:11,635
the judgment that comes afterwards gets

174
00:07:11,635 --> 00:07:15,047
diluted. So it opened five subagents, one

175
00:07:15,047 --> 00:07:17,385
paper each, and asked them to report only

176
00:07:17,385 --> 00:07:20,497
a summary. While they read, the main agent

177
00:07:20,497 --> 00:07:23,530
did other work. It pulled out the figures,

178
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:25,942
and recorded the five filenames exactly.

179
00:07:26,685 --> 00:07:28,672
One of those filenames holds an unusual

180
00:07:28,672 --> 00:07:30,547
dash, so it wrote that one into the

181
00:07:30,547 --> 00:07:32,410
registry with a script rather than by

182
00:07:32,410 --> 00:07:35,185
hand. It also reminded itself of

183
00:07:35,185 --> 00:07:38,322
something. A summary is no substitute for

184
00:07:38,322 --> 00:07:41,022
the original. When it comes to writing the

185
00:07:41,022 --> 00:07:42,967
entry, go back to the paper.

186
00:07:43,710 --> 00:07:45,572
All of this dispatching is handled by the

187
00:07:45,572 --> 00:07:47,897
main agent. You don't have to

188
00:07:47,897 --> 00:07:48,992
arrange any of it.

189
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:52,680
The five came back with three questions.

190
00:07:53,422 --> 00:07:55,705
One of the papers is a perspective piece.

191
00:07:56,447 --> 00:07:59,222
No methods, no results, so the six-section

192
00:07:59,222 --> 00:08:01,617
structure from last episode doesn't fit.

193
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:05,672
What now? Another paper has ten figures in

194
00:08:05,672 --> 00:08:08,472
its appendix. Does each one need a full

195
00:08:08,472 --> 00:08:11,297
record? And the figure extraction tool

196
00:08:11,297 --> 00:08:12,980
failed on almost all five.

197
00:08:13,722 --> 00:08:15,242
You gave three rulings.

198
00:08:15,985 --> 00:08:17,635
The first thing it did was write those

199
00:08:17,635 --> 00:08:19,772
three into the skill, and only then start

200
00:08:19,772 --> 00:08:22,517
work. That order is deliberate.

201
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:25,517
Patch the rules after all five are done,

202
00:08:25,622 --> 00:08:27,705
and the middle four each go their own way,

203
00:08:27,835 --> 00:08:28,847
which is very hard to

204
00:08:28,847 --> 00:08:30,330
reconcile afterwards.

205
00:08:31,708 --> 00:08:33,928
The point of this part is one sentence.

206
00:08:34,670 --> 00:08:36,315
Rules are not written at the end.

207
00:08:37,058 --> 00:08:38,458
They are written the moment you hit the

208
00:08:38,458 --> 00:08:41,258
wall. And this collision means something

209
00:08:41,258 --> 00:08:44,158
extra. When we packaged the skill last

210
00:08:44,158 --> 00:08:46,240
episode, we asked for one thing:

211
00:08:46,383 --> 00:08:48,728
it must not be tied to any single field.

212
00:08:49,470 --> 00:08:51,370
But all six papers then had the same

213
00:08:51,370 --> 00:08:53,703
shape, so that was never really tested.

214
00:08:54,445 --> 00:08:56,470
This time the papers came from completely

215
00:08:56,470 --> 00:08:58,620
different fields, in completely different

216
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:01,908
formats. It needed repair, and that is

217
00:09:01,908 --> 00:09:04,833
normal. A rulebook that real work keeps

218
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:06,020
changing is a rulebook

219
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:07,215
that is still alive.

220
00:09:08,591 --> 00:09:10,604
With the five done, the knowledge base

221
00:09:10,604 --> 00:09:12,716
went from one domain to three, and from

222
00:09:12,716 --> 00:09:15,979
five topics to nine. One paper stayed in

223
00:09:15,979 --> 00:09:17,791
the existing domain and opened a new

224
00:09:17,791 --> 00:09:20,716
topic. Three of them formed a new domain

225
00:09:20,716 --> 00:09:23,666
together. And one connected to nothing at

226
00:09:23,666 --> 00:09:26,011
all, so it became a domain of its own.

227
00:09:26,754 --> 00:09:28,374
More important than the counts:

228
00:09:28,429 --> 00:09:30,479
three older topic pages had their text

229
00:09:30,479 --> 00:09:33,416
revised. One of the new papers uses a

230
00:09:33,416 --> 00:09:35,354
method that can be turned back on an older

231
00:09:35,354 --> 00:09:38,304
conclusion. That is the earlier rule in

232
00:09:38,304 --> 00:09:41,466
action. One paper arrives, and more

233
00:09:41,466 --> 00:09:42,824
than one page moves.

234
00:09:44,203 --> 00:09:46,003
Once there was more in it, you looked at

235
00:09:46,003 --> 00:09:47,698
the index and didn't like it.

236
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,710
Three points. The table is too crowded.

237
00:09:52,453 --> 00:09:55,128
Make it a list. And drop the source

238
00:09:55,128 --> 00:09:58,073
column. The reasoning is practical.

239
00:09:58,815 --> 00:10:00,878
That column holds years, and once there

240
00:10:00,878 --> 00:10:03,160
are many papers a year tells you nothing.

241
00:10:03,903 --> 00:10:06,735
Worse, the first row said all six papers,

242
00:10:06,778 --> 00:10:08,128
which was wrong the day the seventh

243
00:10:08,128 --> 00:10:11,465
arrived. The AI made the change, then did

244
00:10:11,465 --> 00:10:14,278
one extra step. It stripped the other

245
00:10:14,278 --> 00:10:15,878
numbers that will expire out of the

246
00:10:15,878 --> 00:10:18,340
surrounding text, and wrote the new format

247
00:10:18,340 --> 00:10:21,378
into the rulebook. Fixing a page fixes

248
00:10:21,378 --> 00:10:24,760
today. Fixing the rulebook fixes tomorrow.

249
00:10:26,126 --> 00:10:28,421
Next you asked for a changelog page.

250
00:10:29,164 --> 00:10:31,501
The AI went to the work log first and used

251
00:10:31,501 --> 00:10:33,576
it as raw material, but it kept the two

252
00:10:33,576 --> 00:10:36,959
apart. The work log records conflicts,

253
00:10:37,001 --> 00:10:39,571
rejected plans, things tried and dropped.

254
00:10:40,314 --> 00:10:42,321
That is for whoever maintains this.

255
00:10:43,064 --> 00:10:45,401
The changelog records which papers came in

256
00:10:45,401 --> 00:10:47,046
and how the structure changed.

257
00:10:47,789 --> 00:10:49,409
That is for whoever reads it.

258
00:10:50,151 --> 00:10:52,001
Mix them and neither reader finds what

259
00:10:52,001 --> 00:10:54,901
they came for. And notice what adding a

260
00:10:54,901 --> 00:10:57,959
page really takes. Three steps.

261
00:10:58,701 --> 00:11:01,496
Write the page. Add a way in.

262
00:11:02,239 --> 00:11:04,226
And write it into the rulebook so it keeps

263
00:11:04,226 --> 00:11:07,701
going by itself. Skip that third step, and

264
00:11:07,701 --> 00:11:09,996
next month the page still stops at today.

265
00:11:11,362 --> 00:11:13,507
The last job is finishing the links.

266
00:11:14,249 --> 00:11:16,487
Your version was: I had a look, there

267
00:11:16,487 --> 00:11:18,912
could be more links. That is an

268
00:11:18,912 --> 00:11:22,294
impression. The AI turned it into a table.

269
00:11:23,037 --> 00:11:25,924
Topic to topic, topic to entry, entry back

270
00:11:25,924 --> 00:11:28,419
to topic, all four directions laid out.

271
00:11:29,162 --> 00:11:31,324
And once the table existed, the gap was

272
00:11:31,324 --> 00:11:34,299
obvious. The six entries written before

273
00:11:34,299 --> 00:11:36,424
the topic pages linked back to a topic

274
00:11:36,424 --> 00:11:39,782
zero times. Nobody was being lazy.

275
00:11:40,524 --> 00:11:42,499
That is the inevitable result of entries

276
00:11:42,499 --> 00:11:44,757
coming first and topics coming later.

277
00:11:45,499 --> 00:11:47,649
After filling the gaps, it ran the same

278
00:11:47,649 --> 00:11:49,874
table again, to confirm the links were

279
00:11:49,874 --> 00:11:51,494
there and nothing was broken.

280
00:11:52,878 --> 00:11:54,665
There is one more detail in how those

281
00:11:54,665 --> 00:11:57,315
links were written. Every link carries a

282
00:11:57,315 --> 00:11:59,298
sentence saying why it is related.

283
00:12:00,040 --> 00:12:02,428
At eleven entries, a list of links with no

284
00:12:02,428 --> 00:12:03,948
reasons is still readable.

285
00:12:04,690 --> 00:12:06,535
At fifty, it is noise.

286
00:12:07,278 --> 00:12:09,548
That format went into the skill as well,

287
00:12:09,578 --> 00:12:11,198
together with one more rule.

288
00:12:11,940 --> 00:12:14,040
An older entry that this one links to has

289
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:16,753
to link back. Then the next paper

290
00:12:16,753 --> 00:12:18,060
doesn't add to the debt.

291
00:12:19,441 --> 00:12:21,116
The last move of the episode is a

292
00:12:21,116 --> 00:12:22,649
follow-up question from you.

293
00:12:23,391 --> 00:12:25,204
It had said the format is already written

294
00:12:25,204 --> 00:12:27,124
into the skill, so you asked:

295
00:12:27,141 --> 00:12:28,511
did you write a new skill?

296
00:12:29,254 --> 00:12:32,391
The answer was no. It edited the existing

297
00:12:32,391 --> 00:12:35,504
one, in two places. But the question

298
00:12:35,504 --> 00:12:37,204
produced something more useful than the

299
00:12:37,204 --> 00:12:40,674
answer. The AI drew a boundary of its own.

300
00:12:41,416 --> 00:12:43,241
The rules you need while writing an entry

301
00:12:43,241 --> 00:12:44,561
belong in that skill.

302
00:12:45,304 --> 00:12:47,354
Checking links across the whole library is

303
00:12:47,354 --> 00:12:49,429
a different job, and it should wait until

304
00:12:49,429 --> 00:12:51,286
it really happens more than once.

305
00:12:52,029 --> 00:12:53,449
Then it volunteered a gap.

306
00:12:54,191 --> 00:12:56,254
How a topic page gets written is not in

307
00:12:56,254 --> 00:12:59,449
any rule. It only lives in the work log.

308
00:13:00,191 --> 00:13:02,241
A rule that lives only in the log is not a

309
00:13:02,241 --> 00:13:04,954
rule. So in the end, that went

310
00:13:04,954 --> 00:13:06,149
into the skill too.

311
00:13:07,533 --> 00:13:09,153
Three things in this episode.

312
00:13:09,895 --> 00:13:12,390
First, the structure is set by a person.

313
00:13:13,133 --> 00:13:15,383
The AI offers the options, measures the

314
00:13:15,383 --> 00:13:16,915
evidence and does the work.

315
00:13:17,658 --> 00:13:19,833
But two layers or three, and what things

316
00:13:19,833 --> 00:13:21,390
are called, is your call.

317
00:13:22,133 --> 00:13:24,353
Second, update needs a definition.

318
00:13:25,095 --> 00:13:28,183
An inbox, a registry, one small tool, and

319
00:13:28,183 --> 00:13:30,178
four steps written into the rulebook.

320
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:32,870
After that, one word starts the whole

321
00:13:32,870 --> 00:13:36,158
procedure. Third, fixing a page fixes

322
00:13:36,158 --> 00:13:39,540
today. Fixing the rulebook fixes tomorrow.

323
00:13:40,912 --> 00:13:42,625
Which brings us back to the question we

324
00:13:42,625 --> 00:13:45,462
started with. What separates a pile of

325
00:13:45,462 --> 00:13:48,150
files from a wiki? Not the number of

326
00:13:48,150 --> 00:13:50,582
files, and not the software you use.

327
00:13:51,325 --> 00:13:53,175
It is whether anything else has to move

328
00:13:53,175 --> 00:13:54,557
when something new arrives.

329
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:57,562
The middle layer, and that rule about one

330
00:13:57,562 --> 00:13:59,750
paper touching more than one page, are

331
00:13:59,750 --> 00:14:00,945
what make that happen.

332
00:14:01,687 --> 00:14:04,032
Last episode your key move was asking.

333
00:14:04,775 --> 00:14:06,645
This episode, it is ruling.

334
00:14:07,387 --> 00:14:08,975
What they have in common is that both

335
00:14:08,975 --> 00:14:11,195
happen before the AI starts working.

336
00:14:12,564 --> 00:14:14,239
At this point the knowledge base holds

337
00:14:14,239 --> 00:14:16,334
eleven papers and nine topics.

338
00:14:17,076 --> 00:14:18,901
Next episode we use it the other way

339
00:14:18,901 --> 00:14:21,589
round. We build a new course out of what

340
00:14:21,589 --> 00:14:24,739
is inside it. How a topic page becomes a

341
00:14:24,739 --> 00:14:26,976
syllabus, how an entry becomes lecture

342
00:14:26,976 --> 00:14:29,439
material, what the AI can generate on its

343
00:14:29,439 --> 00:14:31,271
own, and what still needs you.

344
00:14:32,014 --> 00:14:33,271
See you next time.
